WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:07.189
Gigantic robot arms glide swiftly along tracks, quickly and efficiently welding pieces together.

2
00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:17.450
Workers step in and around the machines, installing dashboards, windshields, and hundreds of other items, as the partially assembled cars move ceaselessly through the factory.

3
00:00:17.451 --> 00:00:24.831
This is the modern assembly line, and the first man to envision it was Henry Ford, founder of the Ford Motor Company.

4
00:00:24.832 --> 00:00:30.315
Henry Ford was born in 1863 on his family's farm in Dearborn, Michigan.

5
00:00:30.316 --> 00:00:38.664
As a young man, Ford showed more interest in mechanics than farming and spent his time maintaining and improving the farm's machines.

6
00:00:38.665 --> 00:00:43.529
At sixteen, he left the family farm to work as an apprentice machinist.

7
00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.428
Later, he was employed by both Westinghouse and the Edison Illuminating Company.

8
00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:53.813
Ford's early attempts to create an automotive company were fraught with difficulty.

9
00:00:53.814 --> 00:01:01.132
His first company went bankrupt in 1899, and investors forced him out of his second company in 1901.

10
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:03.513
His third attempt was successful.

11
00:01:03.514 --> 00:01:12.582
The Ford Motor Company was formed in 1903, and within ten years, it was one of the dominant players in the early American auto industry.

12
00:01:12.583 --> 00:01:18.407
After Ford introduced the moving assembly line, his company surged ahead of all its competitors.

13
00:01:18.408 --> 00:01:25.013
By 1918, over half the cars sold in the United States were made by the Ford Motor Company.

14
00:01:25.014 --> 00:01:29.454
In the early twentieth century, the assembly line was a new concept.

15
00:01:29.455 --> 00:01:39.545
Previously, when complicated manufacturing was required, a small group of highly skilled workers would hover over the product, working together until it was completed.

16
00:01:39.546 --> 00:01:41.997
An assembly line is a different matter.

17
00:01:41.998 --> 00:01:52.720
Instead of a small number of skilled workers building an entire machine, an assembly line uses a large number of unskilled workers, each concentrating on a single task.

18
00:01:52.721 --> 00:02:01.631
The first assembly lines were less sophisticated than those of today and were used to make only simple products that required few steps to complete.

19
00:02:01.632 --> 00:02:10.353
The product was moved "down the line" by the workers themselves, who carried or pushed the product to the next workstation once their task was finished.

20
00:02:10.354 --> 00:02:16.402
In 1913, Henry Ford embraced a new idea called the moving assembly line.

21
00:02:16.403 --> 00:02:25.840
Conveyor belts moved the product down the line so that Ford's workers no longer wasted time moving from place to place or going back and forth for parts and tools.

22
00:02:25.841 --> 00:02:30.337
"Move the work, not the workers" became the motto of the Ford Motor Company.

23
00:02:30.338 --> 00:02:39.281
Just as streams connect to a river, multiple assembly lines converged with the main line, and parts arrived in time for workers to install them.

24
00:02:39.282 --> 00:02:42.170
Each worker had a very specific task.

25
00:02:42.171 --> 00:02:45.823
One worker would install the steering wheels and nothing else.

26
00:02:45.824 --> 00:02:52.204
Another worker would install only the cars' doors, while another would install just the door handles.

27
00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:55.704
The moving assembly line was an amazing success.

28
00:02:55.705 --> 00:03:01.552
Prior to adopting this strategy, a Ford factory needed over half a day to complete a car.

29
00:03:01.553 --> 00:03:07.188
After adopting the moving assembly line, a car could be manufactured in an hour and a half.

30
00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:18.756
Ford's success is even more astounding when you consider that rather than rely on outside suppliers, he manufactured most of the 1,200 or more parts that went into his cars himself.

31
00:03:18.757 --> 00:03:22.304
Ford's greatest success was undoubtedly the Model T.

32
00:03:22.305 --> 00:03:28.247
Ford first began producing the Model T in 1908, before he adopted the assembly line.

33
00:03:28.248 --> 00:03:34.177
By the end of 1913, the Model T was the best-selling car in the United States.

34
00:03:34.178 --> 00:03:37.558
Ford's strategy was the opposite of his competitors'.

35
00:03:37.559 --> 00:03:46.336
By making his factories as efficient as possible, Ford was able to mass-produce goods that could then be sold at the lowest possible cost.

36
00:03:46.337 --> 00:03:54.448
Each car was sold at a small profit, but Ford sold so many cars that, overall, his company was the most profitable.

37
00:03:54.449 --> 00:04:00.344
The price of the Model T was deliberately kept low, sometimes at the expense of style.

38
00:04:00.345 --> 00:04:04.880
It was famed for being "available in any color, so long as it's black."

39
00:04:04.881 --> 00:04:08.925
Black paint was cheaper and dried much faster than other colors.

40
00:04:08.926 --> 00:04:14.811
As sales of the Model T grew, Ford was able to sell the car at lower and lower prices.

41
00:04:14.812 --> 00:04:23.249
At a time when a reliable car typically cost $1,500, a Model T could be bought for $825.

42
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:29.422
By the time the Model T was replaced by the Model A, more than fifteen million had been sold.

43
00:04:29.423 --> 00:04:32.799
The work of an assembly line employee is not easy.

44
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:40.791
There is little job satisfaction to be found standing in front of a conveyor belt, attaching the same part to another, hour after hour.

45
00:04:40.792 --> 00:04:45.780
Henry Ford understood this problem and sought to improve the lives of his workers.

46
00:04:45.781 --> 00:04:53.724
In 1914, soon after he introduced the assembly line, Ford reduced the length of his workers' shifts to eight hours.

47
00:04:53.725 --> 00:04:58.834
Many factories at that time had their employees work ten-hour shifts every day.

48
00:04:58.835 --> 00:05:04.626
He also raised the minimum day's pay from $2.34 to $5.00.

49
00:05:04.627 --> 00:05:12.380
Doubling the salary of his employees was a move that stunned Ford's competitors, but Ford won the loyalty of his employees.

50
00:05:12.381 --> 00:05:17.211
In 1926, Ford again reorganized his workforce.

51
00:05:17.212 --> 00:05:22.424
Ford's employees went from a six-day workweek to a five-day, forty-hour workweek.

52
00:05:22.425 --> 00:05:29.916
By the standards of the time, Ford's employees were very well paid and had a generous amount of time to spend with their families.

53
00:05:29.917 --> 00:05:33.932
Some historians claim that Henry Ford "invented the weekend."

54
00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:37.581
Ford's goal was not just to make his company profitable.

55
00:05:37.582 --> 00:05:41.499
Henry Ford said, "I will build a motor car for the multitude.

56
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:51.858
It will be so low in price that no man making a good salary will be unable to own one─and enjoy with his family the blessing of pleasure in God's greatest open spaces."

57
00:05:51.859 --> 00:06:00.287
Ford was not just building cars; he was trying to improve the wealth of the country so that employees could afford to buy the goods they helped build.

58
00:06:00.288 --> 00:06:04.498
He hoped to make every American wealthier, not just himself.

59
00:06:04.499 --> 00:06:11.163
If Henry Ford were alive today, he would recognize the modern assembly line as a descendant of his design.

60
00:06:11.164 --> 00:06:15.883
The biggest change since Ford's time is the use of computers and robots.

61
00:06:15.884 --> 00:06:21.223
Robots can lift and lower heavy parts into place with great safety and accuracy.

62
00:06:21.224 --> 00:06:24.877
Ford would also have admired "just in time" inventory.

63
00:06:24.878 --> 00:06:30.916
In the past, millions of dollars were spent keeping large amounts of inventory on the factory site.

64
00:06:30.917 --> 00:06:41.235
Today, suppliers and manufacturers work together to supply needed parts just in time so that manufacturers do not need to warehouse a great number of parts.

65
00:06:41.236 --> 00:06:47.965
The future for car manufacturers appears to be one in which the assembly line becomes increasingly flexible.

66
00:06:47.966 --> 00:06:56.736
Today's most sophisticated production lines can shift from making one type of car to another in order to quickly meet changing customer demands.

67
00:06:56.737 --> 00:07:00.408
Another growing trend is custom manufacturing.

68
00:07:00.409 --> 00:07:07.842
With the help of computers and automated assembly lines, products can be assembled to meet each customer's individual taste.

69
00:07:07.843 --> 00:07:21.865
The future factory will be as efficient as Ford would want, and it will offer greater choice to consumers─a variety beyond the dreams of the man who manufactured a car that was "available in any color, so long as it's black."

